// WQL — ARITHMETIC OVER A VALUE THAT CANNOT SURVIVE THE EL's i64 TOWER IS
// REFUSED, NOT REINTERPRETED.
//
// The EL's checked integer arithmetic is `el_add`/`el_sub`/`el_mul`/`el_div`/
// `el_rem` — i64 in, i64 out, with an overflow returned as an `ElError` rather
// than trapped. So EVERY integer arithmetic node computes in i64 whatever the
// query named its operands, and each operand is normalized into i64 to reach the
// call. For u8..u32 / i8..i32 / isize that normalization keeps every value and the
// arithmetic is ADMITTED (wql_arith_narrow_e2e). For `u64`/`usize` it
// REINTERPRETS and for `u128`/`i128` it TRUNCATES.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS REPLACES IS NOT A WEAKER CHECK, IT IS NO CHECK. MEASURED at the
// parent commit, `from src s select s.v + 1` over a `u64` column:
//   <metaprog-blob-subst>:1: error [fn p_run]: call to 'el_add' arg 1:
//     expected i64, got u64
//   <metaprog-blob-subst>:1190: error [fn p_run]: method 'Vec__push' arg 1:
//     expected u64, got i64
// — two type errors in generated lines, naming neither the column nor the reason,
// for a query the compiler had decided to accept. The same query over a `u32`
// column failed identically; it compiles and answers now.
//
// ⚠ REFUSED AT THE OPERAND, WHICH IS WHERE THE CAST IS. The same fact
// (`ElTy.fits`) refuses a lossy UDF RESULT at its call site
// (wql_udf_wide_int_ret_fail); this is the other site the same `as i64` is
// emitted at, and the two share the ground text rather than a second copy of it.
//
// A `u64` column may still be projected, sorted by, grouped by and compared —
// none of those go through the i64 tower (wql_key_own_type_e2e).
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

struct Rec { pub id: i64, pub v: u64 }

pub deem shifted(src: &[Rec]) { from src s select s.v + 1 }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
